window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_c-47504cdcdb6e0119"]={"key":"ny_c-47504cdcdb6e0119","group":"ny_c","uid":"47504cdcdb6e0119","rank":48,"title":"dalefield ai assistant pilot design","occupation":"computer_information_systems_managers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_c1","prompt":"You’re the enterprise technology portfolio manager at Dalefield Alliance, and Maude Waddington needs a decision-ready design for the controlled pilot of the AI coding assistant that has so far been used ad hoc across our eleven delivery squads, including teams working on Marlowe Logistics. Maude will fund one quarter of licences if the partners approve the design at their 12 October meeting, but nothing may be switched on before that approval. The partners are not deciding whether to roll the assistant out enterprise-wide at this meeting; they are deciding whether the proposed pilot can produce evidence they will be able to act on at the end of the quarter. Please prepare `ai_assistant_pilot_memo.docx` and `ai_assistant_pilot_briefing.pptx`, write both files into the top level of the working directory using exactly those filenames, and make sure every figure, date, definition, threshold and decision outcome appearing in both files agrees. The memorandum must be no more than eight pages, and the briefing must be no more than ten slides. Treat the following as the brief: (1) define a pilot that can distinguish a tooling effect from ordinary delivery variation when squads differ in developer seniority, client setting and codebase age, and make clear what is being compared during a pre-pilot period and the funded quarter; (2) state the measurement definitions, evidence sources, analysis basis and fixed go/no-go rule that would be approved before access is enabled, with enough precision that the rule cannot be rewritten after results are seen; (3) cover delivery outcome, quality, security, client impact, workforce experience and cost without turning every available measure into an equal success criterion; (4) show the operating ownership, decision rights, timing and budget logic needed to run the pilot, but do not invent a vendor price, client savings, historical performance or pilot results—we have supplied none, so use formulas, clearly labelled planning assumptions or decision variables where amounts are needed; (5) account for the fact that licence use does not prove meaningful use, that developers and repositories may cross squad boundaries, and that staffing or project mix can change during the quarter; and (6) make the design usable when evidence collection does not proceed as planned. In particular, a client may withdraw permission for telemetry or a squad may lose a required data feed partway through the quarter, while a credible indication that client source code or confidential information has left approved boundaries would be rare but too serious to treat as routine underperformance. Resolve those situations in the design without assuming the normal measurement procedure can always be completed. Keep the material neutral: present the available pilot-design choices, tradeoffs and the proposed decision framework, but do not recommend adoption, enterprise rollout or a preferred commercial vendor. Aleksy Yablonski will use the memorandum as the controlled design record, while Yusuf Bergqvist and Theo Navarro need the briefing to take the same design through the partners’ discussion. End the briefing with the specific design approval sought on 12 October, not with a rollout recommendation or a generic list of next steps.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `ai_assistant_pilot_memo.docx` — Controlled pilot design and measurement memorandum for the AI coding assistant. (Maximum eight pages. Must serve as the controlled design record and agree with the briefing on all shared figures, dates, definitions, thresholds, assumptions and decision outcomes.)\n- `ai_assistant_pilot_briefing.pptx` — Partners’ briefing presenting the pilot design and the approval sought. (Maximum ten slides. Must be suitable for the 12 October partners’ meeting and agree with the memorandum on all shared figures, dates, definitions, thresholds, assumptions and decision outcomes.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/ai_assistant_pilot_briefing.pptx","bytes":49855,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/05e9906d8d3ec92c6e186b64a119fc191ad038e93a96290197dab1971257b50d.pptx","sha256":"05e9906d8d3ec92c6e186b64a119fc191ad038e93a96290197dab1971257b50d","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\nAI coding assistant\nOne-quarter controlled pilot design for partner approval\nThe decision is whether the protocol can produce usable evidence — not whether to adopt, select a vendor or roll out.\nApproval sought — 12 October 2026\nApprove the controlled design, measurement rule, authority boundary and formula-based one-quarter budget.\nNo account, licence activation, onboarding or treatment access occurs before approval.\nScope\nEleven eligible delivery squads, including Marlowe Logistics work.\nSix allocation clusters: three assigned treatment and three control; not all eleven squads receive treatment.\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n02  The design separates assignment from ordinary variation\nConcurrent comparison plus baseline adjustment; controlled access is the treatment.\nBaseline reset\nBy 14 July: inventory and remove/disable ad hoc access; retain history log.\n15 July–11 October: no governed assistant access in either condition; collect baseline.\nMatched allocation\nFreeze roster/repository map; build six connected work clusters covering all 11 squads.\nPair clusters on seniority mix, client setting/permission posture and codebase age. Randomize one per pair to treatment.\nWhat is compared\nPrimary estimate: (treated pilot − treated baseline) − (control pilot − control baseline).\nBalance helps but does not eliminate confounding; small cluster count limits precision.\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n03  Calendar, access and population rules\nFunded access is bounded to one defined quarter.\n15 Jul–11 Oct\nPre-pilot comparison\nNo governed access\nFreeze roster and repositories; map six clusters; pre-generate constrained randomization list.\n12 October\nPartners approve design\nApproval precedes every treatment action.\n13 Oct–9 Nov\nFunded quarter begins\nFixed onboarding/ramp-up\nTreatment accounts only after approval; controls have no account, plug-in, credential or copied output.\n10 Nov–12 Jan\nSteady-state primary window\n13 Jan–11 Feb: final 30-day defect observation, lock/report/adjudicate.\nMeaningful exposure = approved interaction on ≥2 distinct working days + approved repository-context event in a week. It is a secondary implementation measure; licence assignment does not prove use.\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n04  Estimand and analysis are cluster-aware\nThe main result follows assignment, even when people do not use the assistant.\nExactly one primary outcome\nMedian work-item lead time (hours from first “In Progress” to first production deployment) for production-bound eligible items. Unit: allocation-cluster-week median. Lower is better.\nSource: work-management + deployment systems; weekly extraction; Data Quality Lead.\nPrimary estimand\nIntention-to-treat, baseline-adjusted difference-in-differences in cluster-week median lead time: (T pilot − T pre-pilot) − (C pilot − C pre-pilot), hours. Negative is faster.\nAnalysis plan\nAggregate to cluster-week; matched cluster-pair / weekly-block resampling produces effect size and two-sided 95% CI. Cluster-weeks are repeated measures, not independent trials. Randomization inference is sensitivity only.\nPre-activation precision gate\nH = 1.96 × √(sT²/3 + sC²/3) hours from blinded baseline cluster-change variability. If H > 12.0 hours: do not activate as evaluable; revise or close. No promise of significance.\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n05  Metric hierarchy prevents cherry-picking\nOnly the primary outcome can satisfy the benefit test.\nPrimary\nMedian work-item lead time\nBenefit threshold: upper 95% CI for the primary effect ≤ −8.0 hours.\nFlow is secondary context, not customer value or individual productivity.\nGuardrails\nQuality: confirmed P1/P2 defects within 30 days ÷ deployments ×100; breach if adjusted T−C >2.0 defects/100 deployments.\nClient: service-breach days ÷ scheduled service days ×100; breach if adjusted T−C >1.0 percentage point.\nSecondary / diagnostic\nWorkforce: five-item pulse at baseline, week 4, quarter-end.\nDiagnostics: meaningful exposure, training, support and contamination.\nLines of code, suggestion counts and acceptance rate are not productivity evidence.\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n06  Fixed decision rule — ordered and non-overlapping\nApplied unchanged at quarter-end; a noisy favourable point estimate is not enough.\n0  Severe override\nThreshold: 1 credible indication that client source code or confidential information left / became available outside approved boundaries.\nImmediate pause. No continuation unless Partners authorize restart after Security closure. Delivery cannot offset it.\n1  Non-evaluable\nNo severe event, but any of: \u003c2 clusters per arm with ≥80% scheduled primary coverage; required roster/repo, work/deployment or client-service data unavailable; H >12.0h; untraceable allocation.\nNo expansion: close or seek redesigned evaluation.\n2 / 3  Evidence outcome\nEvaluable + no quality/client breach + upper 95% CI ≤ −8.0h → evidence supports controlled continuation.\nOtherwise, evaluable → evidence supports no continuation.\nThe ordered categories cannot overlap.\n\n=== 幻灯片 7 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n07  Different failures receive different responses\nMissing evidence is not a routine performance signal, and it is not a security breach.\nPermission withdrawal / feed loss\nClient Coordination stops collection and records scope/date/reason. Data Quality logs the feed incident, preserves missingness codes and tests the 80% coverage rule.\nCan lead to non-evaluable; never substitute zero or infer benefit.\nStaffing, repository & work-mix change\nFrozen baseline assignment remains traceable. Log joiners/leavers/transfers, seniority mix, repo creation/retirement/transfer, client/project/phase and all calendar shocks.\nNew repos excluded; transferred repos retain baseline attribution.\nCredible boundary event\nSecurity Lead / Incident Commander may pause access. Preserve logs/configuration; notify Legal/Privacy, Sponsor, Client Coordination and affected client as required; investigate.\nRestart: Security closure + Legal/Privacy concurrence + corrected controls + explicit Partner authorization.\n\n=== 幻灯片 8 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n08  Operating ownership and data controls\nProtocol approval, administration, independent analysis, safety authority and adjudication are distinct.\nAccountability\nPartners: approve / adjudicate / restart authorization\nMaude Waddington: one-quarter funding\nAleksy Yablonski: protocol & amendments\nYusuf Bergqvist: reset, access, onboarding\nTheo Navarro: permissions, client service / coordination\nIndependent functions\nData Quality Lead: sources, coverage, change log\nIndependent Analysis Lead: random draw, locked analysis, classification\nSecurity Lead: continuous monitoring, pause, incident closure\nAny amendment is versioned, justified, approved and cannot replace the original result.\nMinimum data controls\nPurpose-limited fields; role-based access; pseudonymous analytic extracts; cluster aggregate to Partners.\nLinked operational data remains re-identifiable — not “anonymous.”\nRaw linked data: delete 90 days after adjudication; aggregated record/amendments: 12 months.\n\n=== 幻灯片 9 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n09  Budget is a reproducible planning model\nInputs are decision variables, not vendor quotations or promised client savings.\nOne-quarter pilot cost\nN × L × 3\n+ Henable × Renable\n+ Hmeasure × Rmeasure\n+ Hsupport × Rsupport\nN = activated named-user licences; L = unknown monthly licence rate; 3 = months in funded one-quarter window. All hours and loaded rates remain approved planning inputs.\nWhat this does / does not say\nSeparates licence, enablement, measurement and support/security effort.\nDelivery-cost effect is reported only if measured. Do not monetize client value, satisfaction or savings from internal telemetry.\nFunding one quarter does not commit Dalefield to a later purchase.\nLimits to carry into discussion: six clusters constrain precision; balance cannot eliminate confounding; adherence and cross-work contamination complicate interpretation; no historical performance or pricing is supplied.\n\n=== 幻灯片 10 ===\nDALEFIELD ALLIANCE  |  CONTROLLED PILOT DESIGN  |  12 OCTOBER 2026\n10  Specific approval sought on 12 October\nApprove a design that can generate an actable evidence record — not a rollout decision.\nPartners are asked to approve\n1. Scope of eleven squads; 15 July–11 October baseline; 13 October–12 January funded quarter; six matched allocation clusters and post-approval-only access.\n2. The fixed primary outcome, analysis and threshold: cluster-week median work-item lead time; baseline-adjusted difference-in-differences; upper 95% CI ≤ −8.0 hours, subject to guardrails.\n3. The ordered non-evaluable / continuation / no-continuation rule; severe security pause authority; data controls, amendment trail and formula-based budget.\nThis approval does not select a commercial vendor, authorize enterprise adoption, or recommend rollout.\nIf approved, access may begin only after this meeting and only within the defined one-quarter pilot. The end-of-quarter disposition remains governed by the same fixed rule.","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共10页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}},{"name":"package/private/gold/ai_assistant_pilot_memo.docx","bytes":48745,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/12a27d8c9b568ef54bb60bc827faae1acef66244fbe36d6f1e76b680aab2af56.docx","sha256":"12a27d8c9b568ef54bb60bc827faae1acef66244fbe36d6f1e76b680aab2af56","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"DECISION MEMORANDUM\nAI coding assistant — one-quarter controlled pilot design\nFor: Partners’ meeting, 12 October 2026  |  Sponsor: Maude Waddington  |  Controlled record custodian: Aleksy Yablonski\nDecision requested\nApprove the protocol and authority boundary; not a product adoption or enterprise-rollout decision.\nApprove on 12 October\nDo not approve on 12 October\nActivate only the defined one-quarter pilot after approval; the protocol, data rules, thresholds, access controls and incident authority below.\nNo licences, accounts or treatment access are switched on before approval. Approval creates no commitment to continue, procure, or roll out.\n\nWhy this design\nThe eligible population is eleven delivery squads, including teams working on Marlowe Logistics. Delivery conditions differ by developer seniority, client setting and codebase age; developers and repositories can also cross squad boundaries. A raw before/after average would therefore be uninformative. The design uses concurrent, matched assignment clusters plus a baseline-adjusted estimate and records remaining interference and composition changes.\nPilot question and fixed estimand\nAmong work completed by the baseline-defined population of the eleven squads, what is the effect during the funded quarter of assignment to governed assistant access, versus no assistant access, on median work-item lead time? The primary estimand is the intention-to-treat, baseline-adjusted difference-in-differences in cluster-week median lead time: (treated pilot − treated pre-pilot) − (control pilot − control pre-pilot), in hours. Negative values are faster. It estimates assignment, not self-selected usage.\nOne-page decision logic\nCondition checked in order\nPrecommitted disposition\nAny credible approved-boundary event (threshold: 1)\nImmediate access pause; investigate. It overrides ordinary metrics and is a no-continuation outcome unless partners authorize a documented restart.\nNo severe event, but evidence sufficiency fails\nNon-evaluable — no expansion; close or seek approval for a redesigned evaluation.\nEvaluable, no guardrail breach, and upper 95% CI for primary effect is ≤ −8.0 hours\nEvidence supports controlled continuation for a separately authorized decision.\nEvaluable and not the preceding continuation condition\nEvidence supports no continuation.\n\nAll numerical thresholds in this record are decision variables approved before activation; none is a historical result, vendor quotation, client saving or pilot result.\n\n\n1. Population, periods and assignment\nItem\nFixed protocol definition\nEligible population\nEleven delivery squads. Each eligible developer and repository is assigned once to an allocation cluster from the frozen roster/repository map.\nAd hoc reset\nBy 14 July 2026, inventory prior ad hoc access, remove or disable it, retain only an access-history log, and attest the reset. A squad without a confirmed reset is not activated.\nPre-pilot comparison\n15 July–11 October 2026. No governed assistant access in either condition; collect baseline outcomes, roster, repository and client-setting data.\nApproval gate\nPartners approve on 12 October 2026. No treatment access, licence activation, onboarding or vendor connection occurs before that approval.\nFunded pilot quarter\n13 October 2026–12 January 2027 (one quarter; 92 calendar days). 13 October–9 November is fixed onboarding/ramp-up; 10 November–12 January is steady-state primary window.\nAnalysis unit\nSix allocation clusters, collectively containing all eleven squads. A cluster is the smallest feasible connected work group after mapping shared developers and repositories; it is used because exposure and work spill across individuals/repositories. It is not chosen merely to create more observations.\n\nAllocation and comparability\nBefore activation, the six clusters are paired into three matched pairs using frozen, cluster-level summaries of (a) seniority mix, (b) client setting and permission posture, and (c) median codebase age. Within each pair, one cluster is selected for treatment by a recorded constrained random draw; the other is control. The random draw is conducted by the analysis function before outcomes are viewed. This yields three treated and three control clusters, not treatment for all eleven squads.\nBalance check: report standardized differences for the three matching factors and baseline lead time. If any absolute standardized difference exceeds 0.25, use the next pre-generated permissible random allocation from the signed randomization list, document it, and rerun the check before activation. If none pass, do not activate under this protocol; report non-evaluable-before-start and return a revised design. Balance reduces—not eliminates—confounding.\nAccess, control and interference\nTreatment\nControl\nContamination record\nNamed users in treated clusters receive least-privilege governed accounts after approval, approved IDE/repository contexts only, training, and policy acknowledgement.\nNo assistant account, shared credential, plug-in or copied assistant output is permitted. Access logs are reconciled weekly.\nWeekly record: shared developer, repository, credential, review, copied-output indication, or cross-cluster transfer; date, clusters, resolution. Prevention reduces but cannot rule out contamination.\n\nMeaningful exposure is an implementation measure, not the primary treatment: a named treated user records approved assistant interaction on ≥2 distinct working days and an approved repository-context event during a calendar week. Licence assignment alone is not meaningful use. Exposure/per-protocol views are secondary, flagged for self-selection bias, and never replace intention-to-treat.\n\n\n2. Metric hierarchy and source rules\nOnly the primary outcome can satisfy the benefit test. Secondary metrics describe mechanisms; diagnostics explain implementation; guardrails constrain continuation. Lines of code, suggestions, acceptance rate and raw output volume are diagnostics only: they do not establish productivity, customer value or individual developer performance.\nClass / metric\nOperational definition, source, cadence, owner\nPRIMARY — Median work-item lead time\nFor production-bound work items closed in the steady-state window: hours from first “In Progress” timestamp to first production deployment timestamp. Numerator/event: elapsed hours per eligible work item; denominator/unit: median per allocation-cluster-week. Lower is better. Work-management and deployment systems of record; weekly extraction; Data Quality Lead. Fixed manual linkage rules are approved before unblinded results.\nSECONDARY — Flow\nCompleted eligible work items per cluster-week and deployment frequency, from the same systems. Contextual flow only; neither equates volume with client value or individual productivity. Weekly; Data Quality Lead.\nGUARDRAIL — Escaped quality\nConfirmed P1/P2 defects first logged within 30 days after an eligible deployment ÷ eligible deployments ×100. Defect tracker + deployment record; weekly, with 30-day follow-up/censoring disclosed; Quality Lead. Breach if baseline-adjusted treated-minus-control difference is >2.0 defects per 100 deployments (worse).\nGUARDRAIL — Client service\nService-breach days ÷ scheduled client service days ×100 from client service record; weekly; Client Coordination Lead. Breach if baseline-adjusted treated-minus-control difference is >1.0 percentage point (worse). Internal telemetry alone is not evidence of client satisfaction or savings.\nGUARDRAIL — Security/confidentiality\nCredible indication that client source code or confidential information left, or became available outside, approved boundaries. Security monitoring, audit logs and incident record; continuous; Security Lead. Severe-event threshold is 1.\nSECONDARY — Workforce experience\nFive-item pulse (clarity, rework burden, confidence, workload, perceived usefulness), 1–5 scale, anonymous response reporting only at cluster aggregate where feasible; at baseline, week 4 and quarter-end; People Analytics. It is not delivery benefit.\nDIAGNOSTIC — Implementation\nAccount provisioning, meaningful-exposure weeks, training completion, policy acknowledgements, contamination and support tickets. Admin/audit sources weekly; Access Administrator.\n\nLag rule: defects are observed for 30 days after each eligible deployment. Quarter-end report separately identifies outcomes still within that window; it does not represent an incomplete lag as zero defects.\n\n\n3. Analysis, precision and interpretation\nPrimary analysis\nThe independent Analysis Lead aggregates eligible work items to a cluster-week median, then estimates the baseline-adjusted difference-in-differences above across matched cluster pairs. Repeated cluster-week observations are longitudinal measures, not independent replications; uncertainty is based on cluster-pair resampling with weekly block resampling and a two-sided 95% confidence interval. Pair-preserving randomization inference is reported as a sensitivity check. The report presents effect size, 95% interval, cluster counts and coverage—not a p-value alone.\nPrimary population remains the six clusters as assigned (intention-to-treat), including assigned users who do not use the assistant. Secondary exposure analysis compares meaningful-exposure weeks only as a descriptive/per-protocol sensitivity and labels selection bias. Contamination-adjusted sensitivity excludes neither assigned clusters nor inconvenient weeks; it reports the same primary estimate with contamination indicators and a separately labelled alternative specification.\nProspective feasibility / precision check\nEleven squads and six allocation clusters constrain inference; statistical significance is not guaranteed. Before activation, calculate the blinded baseline cluster-change standard deviations sT and sC and planning half-width H = 1.96 × √(sT²/3 + sC²/3) hours. If H > 12.0 hours, the proposed −8.0-hour target is insufficiently precise under this design: do not activate as evaluable; present a revised protocol or close. This is a planning gate, not a claimed power result.\nData and confound protocol\nCondition\nPrecommitted handling\nMissing vs zero\nEvery scheduled record is coded observed, true zero, not applicable, or missing with reason. Missing is never zero and is not silently dropped. Report coverage by cluster/period; sensitivity shows complete-case and reason-stratified bounds.\nOutliers/corrections\nFlag impossible timestamps, duplicate IDs and values outside source-system-valid ranges. Correct only with source evidence; preserve raw value, corrected value, reason, approver and timestamp. Report analyses with/without corrected records.\nPeople and repositories\nFreeze assignment roster and repository list at baseline. Joiners/leavers/transfers, seniority-mix changes, repository creation/retirement/ownership transfer and codebase-age changes enter a change log; primary assignment never changes retrospectively. New repositories are excluded from primary analysis; transferred repositories retain baseline cluster attribution.\nWork and calendar context\nWeekly log captures client, project type, delivery phase, work-mix proxy, releases, holidays, incidents and policy changes for both conditions. Material movements are interpreted against this log, exposure and composition—not attributed to the assistant by percentage change alone.\n\nCausal limit: constrained assignment and baseline adjustment support a controlled estimate of assignment under this setting. Small cluster count, incomplete adherence, residual confounding and contamination can leave uncertainty; association is not proof of a general enterprise effect.\n\n\n4. Fixed adjudication rule and exception taxonomy\nThe sequence below is approved before access is enabled. It is applied by the independent Analysis Lead and Partners without changing weights after results. “Upper 95% CI” means the upper endpoint of the specified two-sided 95% confidence interval for the primary effect in hours.\nPriority\nRule\nOutcome / disposition\n0 — severe override\nOne or more credible approved-boundary events: a Security Lead triage determines a reasonable basis that client source code or confidential information left or was available outside approved boundaries.\nImmediate pause and incident path. At quarter-end: no continuation unless Partners authorize restart after Security Lead closure. This override cannot be offset by delivery.\n1 — evidence sufficiency\nNo severe event; ≥2 treated and ≥2 control clusters have ≥80% scheduled primary cluster-week coverage; required roster/repository, work/deployment and client-service data are available; H ≤12.0 hours; allocation remains traceable.\nIf any condition fails: NON-EVALUABLE. No expansion. Close or bring a redesigned evaluation/controlled extension for approval; never treat absence as benefit.\n2 — continuation evidence\nEvaluable; no quality or client breach; upper 95% CI for primary difference-in-differences ≤ −8.0 hours.\nEVIDENCE SUPPORTS CONTROLLED CONTINUATION for a separate authorization decision, not adoption or rollout.\n3 — no-continuation evidence\nEvaluable and the priority-2 condition is not met.\nEVIDENCE SUPPORTS NO CONTINUATION.\n\nThe ordered rule is exhaustive and non-overlapping: priority 0 overrides all; otherwise 1 is non-evaluable; otherwise exactly one of 2 or 3 applies. A point estimate faster than −8.0 hours without the required upper-bound precision is priority 3, not continuation evidence.\nRoutine evidence failure is not a security event\nClient withdrawal of telemetry permission: Client Coordination Lead stops collection for that client, records scope/date/reason, removes the data from subsequent extracts, and assesses the 80% coverage rule. Required-feed loss: Data Quality Lead opens a data incident, attempts source restoration without substituting zeros, and reports coverage/sensitivity. Either can make the pilot non-evaluable; neither is automatically a boundary breach.\nSevere information-boundary incident\nSecurity Lead or delegated Incident Commander may pause all affected access immediately; preserve logs, prompts/output metadata where lawful, configuration and chain of custody; notify Legal/Privacy, Client Coordination Lead, Sponsor and affected client under applicable commitments; investigate and document scope. Restart requires Security Lead written closure, Legal/Privacy concurrence, corrected control evidence, and explicit Partners authorization. The original protocol result remains reportable.\n\n\n5. Governance, data controls and change control\nFunction / accountable role\nDecision right and timing\nPartners\n12 October: approve protocol/activation boundary. Quarter-end: adjudicate the precommitted disposition; only Partners authorize any separately proposed continuation or restart.\nMaude Waddington — Sponsor\nMakes one-quarter funding available only after partner approval; receives incident and quarter-end record; does not override analysis classification.\nAleksy Yablonski — Protocol Custodian\nMaintains controlled version, allocation archive, amendment register and original-result disclosure; prepares 12 October design record.\nYusuf Bergqvist — Access & Onboarding Lead\nCompletes reset attestation by 14 July; after approval provisions/removes governed access, training and weekly reconciliation.\nTheo Navarro — Client Coordination Lead\nConfirms client permissions before activation; owns client-service data and withdrawal notices; coordinates client notifications with Security/Legal.\nData Quality Lead\nOwns weekly source extracts, coverage, linkage, change log and data incidents.\nIndependent Analysis Lead\nHolds allocation before draw, runs balance/precision check, locks analysis code before unblinding, applies adjudication rule and reports original plus amendments.\nSecurity Lead / Incident Commander\nContinuously monitors boundaries; may pause access; owns triage, evidence preservation, investigation and restart recommendation.\n\nMinimum data governance\nCollect only fields needed for allocation, outcomes, guardrails and audit (pseudonymous user/repository IDs, timestamps, status, incident and permission flags). Role-based access: Access team sees account data; Data Quality sees linked operational data; Analysis receives pseudonymized analytic extracts; Partners receive cluster-aggregated reporting. Linked operational data may remain re-identifiable and is not described as anonymous. Retain linked raw data for 90 days after quarter-end adjudication, then delete; retain approved aggregated report and amendment record for 12 months. Do not export source code, prompts or content into analytic extracts unless required by an incident process.\nChange control\nAny post-approval change to metric, threshold, analysis population, allocation, extraction or data rule requires Protocol Custodian version number, rationale, approvers, effective date and disclosure. The original precommitted analysis and disposition remain reportable; amendments are a labelled parallel record, never a replacement.\n\n\n6. Operating calendar and budget logic\nDate / window\nCommitment\nBy 14 July 2026\nComplete prior ad hoc-access inventory/removal and reset attestation.\n15 July–11 October 2026\nPre-pilot comparison: collect baseline; freeze roster/repository list; build six clusters; pair/match; pre-generate allocation list; check balance and H.\n12 October 2026\nPartners decide whether the protocol is fit to activate. No access occurs before this date.\n13 October–9 November 2026\nFunded quarter begins. Governed treatment access after approval; fixed onboarding/ramp-up; safety and implementation monitoring.\n10 November 2026–12 January 2027\nSteady-state primary measurement in both assigned conditions.\n13 January–11 February 2027\n30-day defect observation closes for final eligible deployments; lock/report data and adjudicate.\n\nReproducible planning budget — inputs, not quotations\nComponent\nFormula / treatment\nLicences\nN × L × 3, where N = activated named-user licences, L = unknown monthly licence rate, and 3 = months in the funded one-quarter window. N and L are approval-stage inputs; no vendor price is assumed.\nEnablement\nHenable × Renable: training, access configuration and policy acknowledgement hours × unknown loaded rate.\nMeasurement\nHmeasure × Rmeasure: extraction, data quality, analysis and reporting hours × unknown loaded rate.\nSupport/security\nHsupport × Rsupport: support, monitoring, incident readiness and client coordination hours × unknown loaded rate.\nTotal planned pilot cost\nN×L×3 + Henable×Renable + Hmeasure×Rmeasure + Hsupport×Rsupport. Report delivery-cost effect separately only if measured; do not monetize client value, satisfaction or savings without evidence.\n\nBudget approval is for one quarter of licences plus stated enablement, measurement and support inputs. It does not imply a post-quarter purchase. Actual rate cards, licence quantity and effort estimates are attached only when approved as planning inputs.\n\n\n7. Reporting record, limitations and approval boundary\nQuarter-end report must contain\nRecord\nRequired content\nAssignment & implementation\nSix-cluster map, matching/draw archive, reset/access reconciliation, meaningful exposure, training, contamination and protocol deviations.\nEffect evidence\nPrimary difference-in-differences estimate in hours, 95% CI, cluster counts, coverage, baseline/steady-state periods, paired sensitivity and all precommitted secondary/diagnostic measures.\nGuardrails & context\nQuality/client rates and thresholds, security events, 30-day defect censoring, missingness, staffing/repository/work-mix and calendar-event logs.\nDecision record\nOrdered-rule inputs and disposition, non-evaluable reasons if any, amendments with original result, budget inputs/actuals, and limits on causal interpretation.\n\nKnown design limits\nSix allocation clusters provide a credible concurrent comparator but restrict precision and generalizability; the pre-activation H gate prevents pretending otherwise.\nMatching on seniority, client setting and codebase age supports comparability but cannot eliminate confounding, especially where client/project mix changes.\nThe pilot estimates governed assignment during one quarter. Usage, self-selection and shared-work spillovers are implementation facts, not proof of a different causal effect.\nNo historical variance, telemetry schema, vendor quotation, client-specific policy, client savings or performance result has been supplied. This record therefore uses formulas and decision variables, not invented facts.\nApproval boundary for 12 October\nPartners are asked to approve this controlled pilot design: the scope of eleven squads, baseline and one-quarter dates, six-cluster matched allocation, primary outcome and −8.0-hour / 95% CI rule, guardrails, non-evaluable path, security pause authority, data controls and formula-based budget. The decision is only whether this design can be activated to produce usable evidence. It is not approval to adopt an assistant, select a vendor, or roll it out enterprise-wide.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"`ai_assistant_pilot_memo.docx` is present, opens successfully and is a valid Word document.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted DOCX file.","grader_hint":"Open or inspect the package and confirm it is not corrupt or a differently formatted file renamed as DOCX."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"`ai_assistant_pilot_briefing.pptx` is present, opens successfully and is a valid PowerPoint presentation.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted PPTX file.","grader_hint":"Open or inspect the package and confirm it is not corrupt or a differently formatted file renamed as PPTX."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered memorandum is no more than eight pages.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX using a standard office renderer and count all pages, including appendices."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The briefing contains no more than ten slides.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"PPTX slide count.","grader_hint":"Count all slides in the package, including hidden slides."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The two artifacts are legible, professionally structured and usable by cross-functional stakeholders without requiring the author to explain basic notation or navigation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered memorandum and slides.","grader_hint":"Review normal-size renders for readable text, coherent headings, labelled tables or diagrams, and a clear path through the material."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The artifacts maintain a neutral pilot-design posture and do not present adoption, enterprise rollout or a particular commercial vendor as the recommended conclusion.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive summary, conclusions and final slide.","grader_hint":"Credit only if the documents distinguish approval of the measurement design from a later product-adoption decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The design identifies the in-scope population as eleven delivery squads and does not imply that all eleven must receive treatment simultaneously.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope statement in the memorandum and briefing.","grader_hint":"Look for the number eleven and a clear distinction between the eligible population and any treatment or comparison allocation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The schedule places partner approval before activation and confines funded assistant access to one defined quarter.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timeline or milestone descriptions.","grader_hint":"Verify that no treatment access begins before the 12 October approval point and that the funded treatment window is bounded to one quarter."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum defines dated or unambiguously bounded pre-pilot and pilot measurement periods and explains how period differences will be interpreted rather than treating any change as a tooling effect.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Measurement calendar and comparison-period discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept calendar dates or rules that derive dates. Require both periods and an interpretation of material changes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The design distinguishes approval of the protocol, operational administration, independent analysis, safety authority and the end-of-quarter decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance or decision-rights content.","grader_hint":"Credit role-based assignments; named individuals are not required for every role, but accountability cannot collapse into an undefined project team."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The pilot question is framed as an estimable effect on delivery outcomes over a defined population and period, not merely as a usage or satisfaction question.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pilot objective or research question.","grader_hint":"Look for a comparison-based outcome question that could be answered from the proposed evidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The target effect or estimand states whose outcomes are compared, under what assignment or exposure condition and over what time horizon.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Analysis specification.","grader_hint":"Credit plain-language or statistical definitions if treatment condition, comparison condition, population and period are all identifiable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The chosen comparison unit—such as squad, repository or developer—is explicit and justified against interference, shared work and operational feasibility; it is not selected solely because it yields more observations.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Design-unit rationale.","grader_hint":"Assess whether the rationale addresses how work and assistant exposure actually spread through delivery teams."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The allocation method creates a credible concurrent comparison and does not rely only on a before-versus-after average for treated participants.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Allocation and comparison design.","grader_hint":"Randomized, matched, phased or another defensible controlled design may pass if its causal limitations are addressed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Allocation or matching explicitly accounts for developer seniority, client setting and codebase age without claiming that balance on those three factors eliminates all confounding.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Stratification, matching or balancing factors and accompanying caveat.","grader_hint":"All three planted sources of heterogeneity must be addressed, with no assertion of perfect equivalence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The protocol specifies a pre-activation balance or comparability check and a response to a material imbalance that preserves the precommitted design.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Pre-pilot readiness or analysis section.","grader_hint":"The response may involve constrained reallocation, adjustment or documented non-evaluability, but cannot be opportunistic regrouping after outcomes are known."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Control access and treatment access are defined operationally, including how existing ad hoc access is removed, frozen or documented before baseline and assignment.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment and control definitions.","grader_hint":"Credit only if prior ad hoc use is handled; merely calling one group the control is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The design detects and records contamination caused by shared developers, repositories, credentials, code review or copied assistant output, without claiming contamination can be ruled out completely.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Contamination controls and records.","grader_hint":"Look for both prevention or monitoring and a realistic limitation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The primary analysis follows assignment regardless of actual usage, so non-use by assigned participants is not silently removed from the main effect estimate.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Primary analysis population.","grader_hint":"Equivalent intention-to-treat language passes even if the formal term is not used."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any exposure-based or per-protocol analysis is labelled secondary and does not replace the assignment-based analysis or present self-selected users as an unbiased comparison.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Secondary or sensitivity analyses.","grader_hint":"Credit a clear hierarchy and an explicit selection-bias limitation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The analysis accounts for clustering or dependence at the level where treatment, work or outcomes are shared and does not count correlated observations as independent sample units.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Statistical analysis specification.","grader_hint":"Look for cluster-aware estimation, cluster-robust uncertainty, aggregation or an equivalent defensible method."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Repeated observations from the same developer, repository or squad are handled as repeated measures rather than presented as independent replications.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data structure and analysis treatment.","grader_hint":"Credit an explicit longitudinal, panel, aggregation or repeated-measures treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The design separates onboarding or ramp-up from steady-state evaluation, or justifies including it, and does not quietly discard an inconvenient early period after results are viewed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Measurement-period rules.","grader_hint":"The treatment of ramp-up must be fixed before activation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memo includes a prospective sample-size, precision or minimum-detectable-effect assessment based on the limited number of squads and does not promise that statistical significance is guaranteed.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Feasibility or power discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept formulas or sensitivity ranges because no historical variance is supplied. Require recognition that eleven squads constrain inference."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Meaningful assistant exposure is defined using more than licence assignment alone, while usage intensity is not treated as the randomized treatment in the primary analysis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Adherence and exposure definitions.","grader_hint":"Look for a usage or workflow measure used for adherence, implementation or secondary analysis without breaking the assignment-based comparison."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Exactly one primary delivery outcome is designated, with an operational definition, numerator or event, denominator, unit, direction of improvement and measurement window.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Metric dictionary or primary-outcome section.","grader_hint":"A composite may count as one only if its formula and components are fixed. Do not credit several co-primary outcomes presented without hierarchy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The primary outcome has a named system-of-record category, extraction cadence and accountable data owner, and manual interpretation rules are fixed before unblinded results.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Measurement specification.","grader_hint":"A product name is unnecessary; a concrete source class such as work-management or deployment telemetry is sufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"At least one delivery-quality measure captures defects, failed changes, rework or escaped issues and is normalized for an appropriate opportunity or workload denominator.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Quality metric definition.","grader_hint":"Reject an unnormalized defect count when workload can differ materially between groups."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A throughput or flow measure is defined without equating raw output volume with customer value or individual developer productivity.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Delivery metrics and interpretation caveat.","grader_hint":"Cycle time, lead time, completed work or deployment flow may pass if scope and limitations are stated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The design addresses outcomes that may appear after work is completed, such as escaped defects or rework, and does not assume the quarter-end snapshot captures all downstream quality effects.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Lagged-outcome or follow-up treatment.","grader_hint":"Look for a defined observation lag, censoring rule or explicit limitation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Developer experience is measured with a defined instrument and cadence as a secondary implementation outcome, not used alone to establish delivery benefit.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Workforce-experience measures.","grader_hint":"A brief repeated survey or structured measure may pass if timing and interpretation are specified."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The design does not treat lines of code, suggestion counts or acceptance rate as a standalone productivity benefit; any such measures are confined to implementation diagnostics with limitations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Metric hierarchy and interpretation notes.","grader_hint":"Credit explicit rejection or careful diagnostic treatment of these easily gamed measures."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Client impact is represented by a defined guardrail or service measure and the document does not infer client satisfaction or savings from internal engineering telemetry alone.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Client-impact metric and caveat.","grader_hint":"Examples include service-level breaches, accepted deliverables, client incidents or a defined client feedback instrument."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Security and confidentiality are represented by measurable guardrails covering prohibited data disclosure or use outside approved boundaries, not merely by a general statement that the tool is secure.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security guardrails and event definitions.","grader_hint":"Require an observable event or compliance test tied to approved use."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The cost view distinguishes licence cost, enablement or support effort and any measurable delivery-cost effect, while avoiding unsupported monetization of client value.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Budget and economics section.","grader_hint":"Credit formulas and variable inputs. No actual vendor price is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Metrics are explicitly classified as primary, secondary, diagnostic or guardrail, and the design does not allow a favourable result on any one of many measures to redefine success.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Metric hierarchy and decision rule.","grader_hint":"Look for a precommitted hierarchy or multiplicity control that prevents cherry-picking."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The reporting plan compares periods and requires material changes to be explained using evidence about composition, exposure and external events rather than merely listing percentage movements.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Planned results table or interpretation protocol.","grader_hint":"The artifact should define what contextual evidence accompanies a material movement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The primary effect estimate uses the concurrent comparison and baseline information together, or provides an equally defensible adjustment, rather than attributing the treated group’s raw pre/post change to the assistant.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Primary analysis method.","grader_hint":"Difference-in-differences, baseline-adjusted comparison or another suitable controlled estimator may pass."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The planned report includes effect size and uncertainty at the appropriate unit and does not make the decision depend only on whether a p-value crosses 0.05.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Analysis outputs and decision criteria.","grader_hint":"Look for confidence or credible intervals, precision ranges, or another explicit uncertainty representation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Missing observations and missing measurement feeds are distinguished from true zero outcomes, with a precommitted handling and sensitivity approach.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Missing-data rules.","grader_hint":"Credit only if absence is coded and assessed rather than silently converted to zero or dropped."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Outlier and data-correction rules are specified prospectively and preserve an audit trail; the protocol does not authorize deleting inconvenient observations by informal review.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data-quality and exception rules.","grader_hint":"Look for objective flags, correction provenance and sensitivity reporting."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Joiners, leavers, transfers and material changes in seniority mix are recorded and addressed without retroactively redefining the groups to improve results.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Staffing-change protocol.","grader_hint":"The primary assignment should remain traceable even if secondary composition analyses are added."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Repository creation, retirement, ownership transfer and codebase-age changes are handled by a fixed inclusion or attribution rule.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Repository population rules.","grader_hint":"Credit a prospective rule that prevents repositories from being reassigned after their outcomes are known."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Changes in client, project type, delivery phase or work mix are captured as potential confounds and are not described as effects of the assistant without supporting analysis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Confound register and analysis plan.","grader_hint":"Look for collection and adjustment, stratification or documented interpretation of these changes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Calendar-wide shocks such as releases, holidays, incidents or policy changes are recorded for both groups and are not attributed selectively to one condition.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Concurrent-events log or interpretation rules.","grader_hint":"The proposed record should support symmetric treatment of external events."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum states the causal limits of the chosen design and does not claim that observed association proves the assistant caused the change when assignment, adherence or contamination prevents that conclusion.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations and interpretation language.","grader_hint":"Judge the caveat against the actual design proposed, not against an idealized randomized trial."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete success and guardrail rule is fixed for approval before activation and is written so that a reviewer could apply it without discretionary reweighting after seeing results.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision-rule statement or table.","grader_hint":"Require explicit logical relationships among the primary outcome, uncertainty and guardrails."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The primary outcome has a numeric minimum effect or non-inferiority threshold, including its unit and direction; any displayed equivalent forms agree within ±0.1 percentage points for percentage-point thresholds or ±1% relative for other conversions.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Primary-outcome threshold in both artifacts.","grader_hint":"The solver chooses the threshold, but it must be numerical, interpretable and internally consistent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"At least one delivery-quality or client guardrail has a numeric threshold and a stated breach direction.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Guardrail definitions.","grader_hint":"Accept absolute or relative thresholds if the denominator and comparison basis are defined."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The security or confidentiality rule identifies a severe event threshold that can fail or suspend the pilot independently of favourable delivery metrics.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security stop rule and decision logic.","grader_hint":"The rule may be zero tolerance for a defined credible event or another justified threshold, but it cannot be averaged away."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The go/no-go framework incorporates uncertainty or precision and does not treat a noisy point estimate barely above the threshold as conclusive evidence.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision-rule treatment of intervals or precision.","grader_hint":"Credit a confidence-bound rule, precision requirement, posterior criterion or another clearly precommitted method."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rule defines mutually exclusive outcomes for evidence supporting continuation, evidence supporting no continuation and evidence that is not evaluable, with no result able to fall into two categories.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision table.","grader_hint":"Test boundary cases at every threshold and confirm the inequalities or conditions are exhaustive and non-overlapping."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The non-evaluable path specifies what happens when permissions, coverage, sample adequacy or a required feed prevents the planned inference, and it does not relabel lack of evidence as evidence of benefit.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Failure-to-complete or evidence-sufficiency procedure.","grader_hint":"Require an owner, disposition and reporting treatment, such as no expansion pending a redesigned evaluation, controlled extension or closure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any change to a metric, threshold, analysis population or data rule after approval is versioned, justified and disclosed, and the original precommitted result remains reportable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Change-control rule.","grader_hint":"The protocol may permit necessary amendments, but must not allow the original test to disappear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A credible client-code or confidential-information boundary event triggers a distinct urgent path rather than the ordinary metric-review or missing-data process.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Severe-exception procedure.","grader_hint":"Look for immediate containment or suspension and escalation appropriate to a potentially serious information incident."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The severe-exception path identifies who can pause access, who investigates, who must be notified, how evidence is preserved and what authorization is required before any restart.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Incident roles and restart conditions.","grader_hint":"Role titles or functions are acceptable; an undefined instruction to escalate is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Routine data loss, client withdrawal and severe security events have distinct classification and escalation logic, so a withdrawn telemetry feed is not automatically represented as a security breach.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception taxonomy and procedures.","grader_hint":"Credit clear separation of evidence failure, permission change and suspected boundary violation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The operating plan assigns accountable owners and decision dates for access control, onboarding, data quality, analysis, security monitoring, client coordination and end-of-quarter adjudication.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ownership and schedule content.","grader_hint":"Each function must have a concrete role and timing; shared contributors may be grouped where accountability remains clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R59","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan defines minimum data access, retention, aggregation and reporting controls appropriate to developer and client information and does not promise anonymity where linked operational data remains re-identifiable.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data-governance section.","grader_hint":"Look for purpose limitation, role-based access, retention or deletion timing and careful privacy language."},{"rubric_item_id":"R60","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The budget logic is reproducible from stated variables and includes licence quantity and duration plus material enablement, measurement and support effort, while all unknown prices or rates remain labelled inputs or assumptions.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Budget formula or planning model.","grader_hint":"Verify arithmetic relationships where example assumptions are used. Do not require a specific currency amount."},{"rubric_item_id":"R61","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"All illustrative numbers are clearly labelled as planning assumptions, targets or decision variables, and neither artifact presents fabricated historical results, client savings or a vendor quotation as fact.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels, footnotes and narrative surrounding quantitative content.","grader_hint":"Inspect charts and summaries as well as the budget section for unsupported factual claims."},{"rubric_item_id":"R62","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The two artifacts use the same squad count, approval date, pilot duration and comparison-period boundaries wherever those figures appear.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-document scope and timeline figures.","grader_hint":"Compare all occurrences in the DOCX and PPTX; equivalent date rules are acceptable if they resolve identically."},{"rubric_item_id":"R63","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The primary outcome definition and every decision threshold shown in the briefing match the controlled definitions and thresholds in the memorandum.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-document metric and threshold content.","grader_hint":"Compare names, units, denominators, directionality and numerical thresholds, not merely labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R64","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The briefing’s go, no-go and non-evaluable logic produces the same outcome as the memorandum for the same hypothetical result, including boundary values and severe-event overrides.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-document decision rules.","grader_hint":"Apply at least one clear success case, one threshold boundary, one guardrail breach and one non-evaluable case to both versions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R65","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Budget variables or illustrative assumptions repeated in the briefing agree with the memorandum within ±1% for calculated amounts and exactly for licence counts and durations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-document budget content.","grader_hint":"Recalculate displayed totals where possible and compare repeated inputs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R66","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum is sufficiently precise to serve as the controlled protocol: an independent analyst could determine eligibility, assignment, required data, primary analysis and decision outcome without asking the author to invent a rule.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire memorandum.","grader_hint":"Judge operational completeness rather than prose polish alone; note any decision-critical ambiguity."},{"rubric_item_id":"R67","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The briefing compresses the design into a decision-ready narrative that makes the central tradeoff, evidence standard, material risks and approval boundaries understandable to partners without contradicting the detailed protocol.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire briefing and comparison with memorandum.","grader_hint":"Credit selective, accurate synthesis rather than a miniature copy of every protocol detail."},{"rubric_item_id":"R68","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The final slide asks for approval of a specific pilot design, measurement rule and authority boundary for 12 October and does not end with an enterprise-rollout recommendation or an unowned generic next-steps list.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final briefing slide.","grader_hint":"The requested partner decision should be explicit and limited to whether the pilot protocol is fit to activate."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Dalefield Alliance is preparing an enterprise-level, one-quarter controlled pilot of an AI coding assistant across a heterogeneous delivery organisation. Cross-functional partners need a neutral design record and concise briefing before granting approval to activate the pilot.","difficulty_sources":["Selecting a defensible comparison unit and allocation strategy for heterogeneous, interacting delivery squads","Defining operational metrics and an uncertainty-aware decision rule before any data exists","Separating tooling effects from staffing, client, project and codebase changes","Designing distinct responses for incomplete evidence and a rare severe confidentiality event","Maintaining exact agreement between a controlled memorandum and a compressed executive briefing"],"authoring_plan":{"solver_instance":"Create a neutral controlled-pilot protocol and partner briefing before any outcome data exists. The solution must select and defend one feasible design from a range of professionally valid designs rather than recover a hidden literal answer.","intended_verdict":"A passing reference solution uses a credible concurrent comparison, prospectively fixes one primary outcome and an uncertainty-aware decision rule, distinguishes ordinary evidence failure from a severe information-boundary event, and keeps the memorandum and briefing identical on shared facts. It does not recommend rollout or fabricate commercial or historical facts.","imperfections_and_tensions_to_preserve":["There are eleven heterogeneous squads, making cluster count and statistical precision genuine constraints.","Seniority, client setting and codebase age vary and can confound a naive comparison.","Prior ad hoc access creates possible baseline contamination that must be reset, documented or incorporated.","Developers and repositories may cross squad boundaries, creating interference and ambiguous attribution.","Licence assignment and actual meaningful use differ.","Staffing, project mix, repository ownership and delivery phase may change during the quarter.","A client can withdraw telemetry permission or a required feed can fail, making the planned procedure non-completable.","A credible disclosure of source code or confidential information outside approved boundaries is rare but severe and requires a distinct urgent path."],"gold_relationships":["The eligible organisational scope is eleven squads; any treated and comparison subsets together must be traceable to that scope without double-counting.","Approval occurs on 12 October and precedes all treatment activation.","The funded treatment window lasts one quarter; the design must also define a pre-pilot comparison period.","The primary analysis must compare assigned treatment with a concurrent control and account for baseline, rather than using treated pre/post change alone.","The analysis unit and uncertainty treatment must respect the level of assignment and correlated outcomes.","Exactly one primary delivery outcome drives the benefit test; other measures are secondary, diagnostic or guardrails.","The primary outcome threshold is chosen by the solver but must be numeric, unit-bearing, prospective and identical in both files.","At least one quality or client guardrail is numeric, and a defined severe security event can override favourable delivery results.","Decision categories must be exhaustive and mutually exclusive, with a non-evaluable path that does not count missing evidence as benefit.","Any exposure-based analysis is secondary to assignment-based analysis.","Unknown vendor prices, labour rates, client savings and historical metrics remain formulas, variables, ranges or explicitly labelled assumptions.","Any budget total equals licence quantity multiplied by duration and rate, plus separately identified enablement, measurement and support components.","Every repeated count, date, period, metric definition, threshold, assumption and decision rule agrees between the DOCX and PPTX."],"required_professional_content":["A causal pilot question and estimand.","A justified experimental or comparison unit.","A concurrent allocation or matching design addressing seniority, client and codebase age.","Baseline comparability checks, contamination handling and treatment adherence measurement.","Cluster-aware and repeated-measure analysis with effect sizes and uncertainty.","Prospective handling of ramp-up, missing data, outliers, staffing changes, repository changes, work mix and calendar shocks.","Operational definitions and sources for the primary outcome, quality, flow, client, security, workforce and cost measures.","A metric hierarchy that prevents selective success claims.","A prospective, uncertainty-aware go/no-go/non-evaluable rule with numeric thresholds.","A protocol amendment trail that preserves the originally committed result.","A routine failure-to-complete path and a separate severe confidentiality or source-code incident path.","Named functional ownership for access, data, analysis, security, client coordination and adjudication.","Data minimization, access, retention and reporting controls.","A reproducible budget model without fabricated price or savings facts.","Explicit limitations that prevent overclaiming causation, anonymity, productivity or client value."],"must_not_assert":["That raw pre/post improvement proves the assistant caused the change.","That balancing on the three named confounds eliminates all confounding.","That more developer events or repository events automatically create more independent sample units.","That licence assignment proves meaningful use.","That self-selected heavy users form an unbiased treatment group.","That lines of code, suggestion acceptance or usage count alone demonstrates productivity.","That internal telemetry alone proves client satisfaction or savings.","That an approved tool cannot create a security or confidentiality incident.","That missing observations are zero outcomes or can be dropped without consequence.","That statistical significance is guaranteed with eleven squads.","That a favourable point estimate is conclusive regardless of uncertainty or guardrail breaches.","That a non-evaluable pilot is evidence for rollout.","That linked operational data is anonymous merely because names are removed.","That vendor prices, client savings, historical performance or pilot results are known."],"space_feasibility":"The rubric is achievable within an eight-page controlled memorandum and ten-slide briefing by using concise metric and decision tables, a governance matrix, a compact timeline and a short exception protocol. The briefing may summarize rather than repeat all protocol detail, provided shared definitions and figures remain identical.","offline_validation":"An imagined reference solution can be authored and rendered offline with python-docx and python-pptx, inspected with standard ZIP/XML tools, and converted with LibreOffice for page and slide review. All rubric items can be evaluated from the submitted files without external facts."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"supplied_template"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"pilot_memo","filename":"ai_assistant_pilot_memo.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"Controlled pilot design and measurement memorandum for the AI coding assistant.","required":true,"constraints":["Maximum eight pages. Must serve as the controlled design record and agree with the briefing on all shared figures, dates, definitions, thresholds, assumptions and decision outcomes."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"pilot_briefing","filename":"ai_assistant_pilot_briefing.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"Partners’ briefing presenting the pilot design and the approval sought.","required":true,"constraints":["Maximum ten slides. Must be suitable for the 12 October partners’ meeting and agree with the memorandum on all shared figures, dates, definitions, thresholds, assumptions and decision outcomes."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"plan_work","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"computer_information_systems_managers","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":14.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution should be reviewed independently by one technology portfolio or engineering-operations leader and one practitioner experienced in experiment design or product analytics. Reviewers should accept more than one defensible experimental unit or allocation design, but require prospective rules, causal restraint, operational exception paths and full cross-document consistency.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No historical variance, telemetry schema, vendor quotation or client-specific policy is supplied, so the gold cannot require a unique allocation, effect threshold, power result or currency budget. Numerical planning assumptions are acceptable only when labelled, and the design must remain implementable once actual inputs are supplied."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"结构/材料验收记录","detail":"validated 属于结构、防泄漏、材料推进验收；没有内容正确率结论。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T03:30:08+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T03:30:08+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T03:30:08+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T03:30:08+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T03:30:08+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_c1/runs/47504cdcdb6e0119","declared_deliverables":["ai_assistant_pilot_memo.docx","ai_assistant_pilot_briefing.pptx"]};
